<p>I can see where you're coming from with the meta fatigue; I ended up no-voting this myself because, while it's well executed, clearly worked hard on, and a basic idea that I like, I feel like I've seen this before. &quot;The act of writing SCPs in real life affects the in-universe characters&quot; is a premise that I can agree has been done enough times that, even when done excellently as it is here, I'm not very interested in seeing again.</p>
<p>I disagree that pataphysics as a device is a dead end, though - I think it just hasn't reached its full potential yet because the tendency is still to focus it <em>outward</em>, on the interaction between the in-universe Foundation and its real life writers, rather than on the in-universe characters exploring what it means to be fictional. I think there's a ton of potential in the concept of a super-science organization that can break apart the building blocks of their fictional existence and examine the feelings and tropes their world is made up of in the same way we examine atoms and molecules. My own contest entry was an (admittedly clumsy and poorly thought out) attempt to use pataphysics that way; I think as time goes on, we as a site will figure out how to use this concept better, since SCP is a uniquely qualified setting to explore it, and that it could eventually open up a lot of design space for new stories.</p>

