<p>I appreciate your forthrightness. And I certainly can't criticize you for disliking my work on a conceptual level, that's totally fair.</p>
<p>If you're tired of pataphysics, there's certainly nothing wrong with that. But I will say that describing pataphysics as a &quot;wave&quot; feels a bit hyperbolic to me. Like, I'm thinking about it, and it seems like there are maybe 10 SCPs that deal with the Foundation engaging with it's own fictionality? And a few tales and author pages? That's not that many.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Give it a few months; pataphysics is too much of a gimmick to produce unique content after a certain period, I guarantee it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You may well be right. However, I will say this: When I first discovered the site during the Series II era, I didn't just read SCPs, I often read the discussion pages as well. And there were a lot of people who didn't like Hume fields and Scranton Reality Anchors for, in my view at least, similar reasons to people who don't like pataphysics. They thought it was silly, a gimmick, invalidated conflict, overused, etc. I think pataphysics might surprise you.</p>

