				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="2303803"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1533147916 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Aug 2018 18:25</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3894425">
									

<blockquote>
<p>But I will say that describing pataphysics as a &quot;wave&quot; feels a bit hyperbolic to me.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>All I know is that I've been on this site <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">five</span> 4.5?<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-910702-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-910702-1')">1</a></sup> years, I'm active as hell, and it came out of nowhere a few weeks ago. Maybe I was missing something, or 'active' on the forums and not paying attention to all the recent articles outside of 4000.</p>
<p>But here's an elaboration of why I have issues with these canonical implications. This is dealing with some stuff that's damn hard to put into English but it comes with the topics. I'll try. I am open to people disagreeing with this of course, but I'm hoping that I might be able to summarize some of the conceptual issues that I and maybe other anti-pataphysics people have.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>IMO Humes and SRAs make as much scientific sense as radiation detection does. The sci-fi part is the fact that the <em>technology</em> is currently impossible IRL, but the mechanism makes sense. Create specialized equipment to detect something &#8212;&gt; determine what the equipment's response to various stimuli is &#8212;&gt; develop a unit based upon the response and quantify your observations accordingly.</p>
<p>Pataphysics doesn't hold up in the same way.</p>
<p>Concept: &quot;I, a Foundation employee, mirror the actions of a writer in a reality construct where I am a work of fiction.&quot;</p>
<p>A Multi-U look at this would have them say &quot;I, a Foundation employee, exist here in this reality construct. There exists a reality construct where I am fictional. However, we cannot prove that the author created me, as I have always experienced my life as real, it is real according to my Kant counter, and it is real according to the normality standards my job requires me to enforce.&quot;</p>
<p>A pataphysics approach would be &quot;I, a Foundation employee, exist here in this reality construct, which is actually not real and was written by someone in a different reality construct. They control my actions but somehow I'm still able to have autonomy<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-910702-2" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-910702-2')">2</a></sup> and the best option is to base my actions around the implication that I'm not real.&quot;</p>
<p>The Multi-U approach acknowledges that reality depends upon frame of reference. There could be a reality construct in which a bunch of Eldritch gods are sitting around in their underwear playing chess, and the Foundation finds out that each of their pieces represents a famous Foundation action in history (or some shit; I'm spitballing for the sake of example). They would not all of a sudden define themselves as &quot;welp, I guess we're actually just a game of chess!&quot; Why? Because there could be <em>another</em> all-explaining situation that reveals itself. There are <em>infinite</em> possibilities and <em>infinite</em> universes and there's no reason for the Foundation to treat one as truth just because it sounds believable. How is it any more believable than other trickery brought upon by the existence of infinite realities?</p>
<p>Pataphysics is acknowledging our (IRL) reality construct as more real than the other ones and defining our actions on the wiki as the only real nature of existence for these characters. This is the opposite of the narrative value of having multiple reality constructs, to me.</p>
<p>If we take pataphysics to heart and believe what it says: Now we know what's objectively real. That's it. It's over. Pataphysics says &quot;this is the only option for existence.&quot; Edit: <a href="/metafiction">Metafiction</a> insinuates this too, but I tolerate the conclusion because characters talking reached it, and characters talking are susceptible to panicking and concluding that. The team composing this scientific document and researching this phenomenon, however, should know better than to show the same behavior.</p>
<hr />
<p>Obligatory extra &quot;in my opinion&quot; because I know I just dumped a lot of shit here.</p>
</div>
