			<div class="content" id="post-content-4172038">
									

<p>Hi, I'm gonna throw my hat in on this here a bit.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;bear&nbsp;witness&nbsp;to&nbsp;my&nbsp;pathological&nbsp;ranting</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;TAKE&nbsp;IT&nbsp;AWAY!!!&nbsp;TAKE&nbsp;IT&nbsp;AWAY!!!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>To me, everything Cyantreuse is talking about here is exactly the reason why I.H.P.'s Proposal exists. Yes, most of the &quot;sci-fi&quot; material in the SCP Foundation is based on concepts that make mechanical sense, but are technologically impossible. Humes, like you said, are a great example of this.</p>
<p>Pataphysics (at first glance) does not follow this rule. It isn't based on mechanical logic, and isn't something that could be technologically possible in the future.</p>
<p><em>Or is it?</em></p>
<p>This is exactly the argument that IHP's Proposal makes (and, ironically, we see a similar argument at the end of this article, too). IHP asserts that the Swann line &quot;god is a bunch of horror writers&quot; isn't the end of the story and never was. There is far more to investigate about this subject, and in IHP's Proposal we see a bunch of researchers only <em>beginning</em> to scratch the surface. In 4410 over here, Saxon suggests that some of this stuff might not be anomalous at all, but is instead a natural evolution of the writing process.</p>
<p>In other words, this is uncharted territory. Things like Humes, while foreign, were couched in familiar-enough scientific concepts for Foundation researchers to apply the scientific method. But no one, in our reality or theirs, has stopped to consider the possibility that <em>storytelling might be a measurable universal constant.</em></p>
<p>What if Pataphysics is a form of science? What if writing, like space and time, is a documentable natural phenomenon, and we're just waiting for the Einstein equivalent of fiction writers to come along and paint us the whole picture? It's just my interpretation, but to me that's exactly what articles like this one and IHP are suggesting.</p>
<p>Basically, my point is, I don't think Pataphysics says &quot;this is the only option for existence.&quot; I think what it actually says is &quot;this is <em>an</em> option for existence that we really ought to do some research on, because there's a distinct possibility that calling writing an 'art' is a misnomer.&quot;</p>
<p>As a side note, all the Pataphysics articles I've read have done (in my opinion) a good job of staying vague in the right places. Most of them reference multiple reality constructs that contain the Foundation, and imply that the meta stuff only applies to some of them (or maybe only one of them). Most Pataphysics articles give you a way in-article to disregard it as canon, or otherwise treat it as a canon for a different reality construct.</p>
<p>On a related note, from what I've seen, Multi-U and Pataphysics support each other more than anything else. Both of them use the multiverse concept to full effect. Actually, no sucking up here I swear, one of my favorite meta articles is <a href="/scp-2758">one of yours</a>, for basically this exact reason.</p>
<p>I've definitely gone on long enough, so final thoughts here. To me, one of the core tenants of the SCP Foundation is the mantra that &quot;anything can happen.&quot; Pataphysics is a necessary, even obligatory part of that. &quot;Anything can happen&#8230; yes, <em>even that.</em>&quot; So, to me, as long as Pataphysics continues to respect the Multi-U approach and acknowledge that there's reality constructs where there is no metafiction, then there's no harm in keeping it up.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Anyway, thanks for taking the time to read. No harm intended, just sharing my thoughts.</p>

